Game UI fonts - AXIS, Trump Gothic, Jupiter
-------------------------------------------
Not bundled. Dalamud builds these at runtime from the font files in the user's
own game installation, so no font data from the game is contained in this
repository or in the released plugin. These typefaces are commercial faces
licensed by Square Enix for use in the game and are not redistributable.


Eorzean alphabet font - assets/fonts/Eorzea.ttf
-----------------------------------------------
Included in this repository and in the released plugin, and expressly NOT
covered by the AGPL above.

The file is a fan-made TrueType recreation of the Eorzean alphabet, built with
FontCreator. Its own metadata records:

    Copyright     (C) Eorzea font was created by Square Enix
    Copyright     Original (C) Square Enix.
    Description   Eorzea Font from Final Fantasy XIV. Font originally created
                  by Square Enix. Many thanks to www.eorzeapedia.com and
                  www.ffxivmaps.com for reference materials.

Read carefully, that metadata describes two separate things:

  The alphabet    Square Enix's design, as used in FINAL FANTASY XIV. The
                  copyright line is scoped to the "Original" - it credits the
                  source of the letterforms.

  This TTF file   Drawn by a fan from public reference material, using
                  FontCreator. It is not a game asset and contains no data
                  extracted from the game.

The font's name table has no Designer (id 9) or Manufacturer (id 8) record,
so its author is not identified in the file, and no LicenseDesc (id 13) or
LicenseURL (id 14) record, so no terms are stated by anyone.

This project therefore claims no rights in either layer and grants none to
anyone. The file is included solely so the decode effect can render the
in-game script. Anyone redistributing or forking this project should reach
their own view on whether to carry it.

It is not required. FontService.TryBuildEorzean treats a missing font as a
normal condition, and the reveal falls back to a plain fade — so deleting it
costs the effect and nothing else.


